- Retrieved practices:
- Do not call system() with environment-dependent paths
WHAT: Calling system() with commands that depend on environment variables (for example, HOME) can cause unintended files or resources to be accessed or modified.
WHY: Attackers can manipulate environment variables so that privileged programs execute commands on attacker-controlled paths, leading to arbitrary file deletion or other command execution impacts.
HOW: Avoid system() entirely; instead, implement the required functionality directly using library or OS APIs (for example, file APIs to delete files) and pass explicit, validated paths rather than environment-derived ones.
EXAMPLE (C, POSIX): Before: system("rm ~/.config"); After: use getenv("HOME") safely, validate the resolved directory, construct the full path, and call unlink() on that path without invoking a shell.

- Avoid inappropriate device operations in place of file I/O
WHAT: Performing file operations (open, GetFileType, read, write) on paths that actually refer to devices (e.g., named pipes, COM/LPT, device namespaces) can block indefinitely or trigger device side effects.
WHY: An attacker can cause denial-of-service by forcing blocking operations, or cause unintended hardware/OS behavior when device handles are opened.
HOW: Before treating a path as a file, detect and reject device paths (e.g., reserved names, device namespaces like `\\.\`), and avoid TOCTOU by validating and operating on the same canonical path atomically.
EXAMPLE (C/Windows, before): `h = CreateFile(userPath, ...); type = GetFileType(h);`
EXAMPLE (C/Windows, after): `if (isReservedName(userPath)) fail(); else h = CreateFile(userPath, ...);`

- Avoid unsafe C input functions that can overflow buffers
WHAT: Using gets() or any input function that does not limit the number of characters read into a fixed-size buffer can cause buffer overflows when input exceeds the buffer capacity.
WHY: Buffer overflows can corrupt memory, crash the program, or allow arbitrary code execution.
HOW: Never use gets(); instead, use fgets() with the destination buffer size, then remove the trailing newline if present and flush any remaining input from stdin.
EXAMPLE: Noncompliant: `char buf[1024]; gets(buf);`
EXAMPLE: Compliant: `char buf[1024]; if (fgets(buf, sizeof(buf), stdin)) { char *p = strchr(buf, '\n'); if (p) *p = '\0'; }`

- CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input
WHAT: Failing to allocate enough storage for strings (including the null terminator) or copying strings without checking destination size can cause buffer overflows and off-by-one writes/reads.
WHY: Buffer overflows and out-of-bounds accesses can corrupt memory, leak data, or enable arbitrary code execution (CWE-119, CWE-123, CWE-125, CWE-193).
HOW: Always allocate string buffers with space for all characters plus the null terminator; use size-aware APIs that limit copies to buffer length; validate and constrain tainted input lengths before formatted I/O or string-copy operations.
EXAMPLE (C): // Before: char buf[10]; strcpy(buf, user_input);
// After:  char buf[10]; strncpy(buf, user_input, sizeof(buf)-1); buf[sizeof(buf)-1] = '\0';

- Close files when they are no longer needed
WHAT: Failing to close file handles (for example, not calling CloseHandle on a HANDLE before returning) causes resource leaks and can exhaust available file descriptors.
WHY: Leaked file handles can lead to denial of service, unexpected failures opening new files, and potential data integrity issues.
HOW: Ensure every successful file open/creation has a corresponding close in all code paths (success, error, early return); use structured cleanup (finally blocks, defer, or a single exit path) to guarantee closure.
EXAMPLE: Before (Windows C): `HANDLE h = CreateFile(...); if (error) return -1; /* no CloseHandle */` After: `HANDLE h = CreateFile(...); if (h == INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE) return -1; /* ... */ if (!CloseHandle(h)) return -1;`

- Close files when they are no longer needed
WHAT: Failing to close file descriptors/handles (for example, returning from a function without calling close()/CloseHandle()) causes resource leaks and may exhaust available file handles.
WHY: Leaked file resources can lead to denial of service, unpredictable behavior, and inability to open new files, impacting reliability and availability.
HOW: Ensure every successful open/CreateFile() call has a corresponding close/CloseHandle() on all code paths, including error paths and early returns; use structured cleanup (goto, defer, RAII) to guarantee closure.
EXAMPLE (C, POSIX) BEFORE: `int fd = open(path, O_RDONLY); if (fd == -1) return -1; /* ... */ return 0;`
EXAMPLE (C, POSIX) AFTER: `int fd = open(path, O_RDONLY); if (fd == -1) return -1; /* ... */ if (close(fd) == -1) return -1; return 0;`



